Thermistor Temperature Monitor
An Arduino-based temperature monitoring and control system that measures ambient temperature using a thermistor and displays the current temperature and adjustable setpoint on an LCD1602.
Features
- Measures temperature using a thermistor voltage divider
- Converts analog readings into temperature using the thermistor Beta equation
- Averages 10 readings to reduce measurement noise
- Displays current temperature and set temperature on an LCD1602
- Adjustable temperature setpoint using two push buttons
- Setpoint limited between 15°C and 35°C
- Green LED indicates temperature is below the setpoint
- Red LED indicates temperature is above the setpoint
- Uses hysteresis to prevent rapid LED switching around the setpoint
Hardware
- Arduino Uno
- Thermistor
- 10kΩ resistor
- LCD1602 display
- 2 push buttons
- Red LED
- Green LED
- Breadboard and jumper wires
How It Works
The thermistor and 10kΩ resistor form a voltage divider. The Arduino reads the divider's voltage through an analog input and calculates the thermistor's resistance. The resistance is then converted into temperature using the Beta parameter equation.
Multiple temperature readings are averaged to produce a more stable measurement.
The two buttons allow the user to adjust the temperature setpoint between 15°C and 35°C.
A small hysteresis range prevents the LEDs from constantly switching when the measured temperature is very close to the setpoint.
